rstand what is happening here today  and why this  is so important well it really is  exciting and even if you've covered it  for 30 years like i have it you still  get pretty fired up to watch something  like this a brand new rocket  flying with people on board for the very  first time  of course what this does is is at least  jeff bezos hopes it does  is usher in a new era of commercial  space travel  where passengers can buy tickets board  this rocket fly out of the lower  atmosphere experience a few minutes of  weightlessness  and then return to earth the big  question is can bezos and richard  branson who's competing with him  sustain this market over the long haul  these are not inexpensive flights  only very rich people or governments are  going to be able to put people on board  for a long time so the question is can  they sustain this as a new marketplace  and keep it going long enough for  economies of scale to kick in  and prices to come down  yeah because right now we know about  someone who bid you know  28 million dollars we don't know how  much oliver damon paid the 18 year old  who's on board but he is a paying  customer  but they haven't told us exactly how  much he paid  so can you compare the two flights you  know richard branson went up with great  fanfare came down  was successful we're very glad about  that that is the expectation about what  will happen here today  can you explain the difference between  the two and is it right even to compare  them  yeah you have to compare them because at  the bottom line both  both passengers on both spaceships are  going to get about three minutes of  weightlessness  they're going to be effectively in space  so that's the the bottom line  selling point but how they get there is  very different  you know richard branson is using a very  futuristic looking space plane  it's carried up to 45 000 feet by a  carrier jet and then released  that's when it fires its rocket motor  soars up out of the atmosphere and then  glides back to a runway landing  now jeff bezos has chosen a much more  traditional approach  it's a booster with the capsule on top  if you want to think about it that way  just like when alan shepard blasted off  on the first mercury flight  that was a suborbital flight with a  booster and a capsule on top  so it's very similar in the design  philosophy so they ride their rocket up  they separate from the rocket in the  capsule soar up to altitude  they're going to get some fantastic  views they've got the biggest windows  ever built into a spacecraft and then  they come back down to earth under  parachutes for a desert landing  i know bill everybody keeps talking  about the size of these windows which by  the way is ginormous i have to say  you know and i actually thought it was  roomy in there but why are these windows  such a big deal everybody  the other when we're walking people did  you notice our windows did you notice  our windows they're very proud of those  windows  well they're going to give a fantastic  view i mean there's no question about  that  they're bigger than the largest window  on the international space station if  you want to think about it like that  so if you're paying for the view and  you're three minutes of weightlessness  the bigger the window i guess  the better the view but on the other  hand virgin galactic has more windows  and they're certainly sufficient to look  out and see the earth below so  i think it's it's one of those points  where if you're a car salesman you say  my car has this and the other guy says  mine has that but the big windows are  certainly a selling point no question  but but this is not a competition bill  between billionaires everybody keeps  telling us that  and can you just briefly talk about the  difference between the two you said  virgin galactic the virgin flight once  you're in you're in  on this particular rocket there is an  escape in case god forbid it doesn't go  according to plan  yeah that's that's certainly going to be  a selling point that blue is blue origin  is going to advertise  um you're right the the virgin galactic  space plane is kind of like  when you get on a commercial airliner  you're on board until it lands again and  it's the same case with the virgin  galactic space plane now  they argue that their engine which is  which is  doesn't have to be as powerful as the  blue origin engine because it doesn't  fire until you're up at 45  000 feet they have a way to control that  engine and turn it off if they see a  problem coming in which case  they would just shut the engine down and  glide back to earth but blue origin has  a what they call a full envelope escape  system  meaning anywhere from sitting on the  launch pad all the way to space  the blue origin capsule has its own  rocket motor built in that can fire in a  fraction of a second  so push that capsule away from a  malfunctioning booster and that's  something  people are going to have to think about  all right bill well listen everything's  going to go according to plan today  because these guys are ready now let's  go back to the studio thank you bill

